        "chapters": [
          {
            "id": "edu-chapter-class-10-maths-quadratics",
            "title": "Quadratic equations",
            "summary": "Factorisation and the quadratic formula.",
            "objective": "By the end of this chapter, you can solve any quadratic equation and describe its roots.",
            "status": "published",
            "lessons": [
              {
                "id": "edu-lesson-class-10-maths-quadratic-lecture",
                "title": "Quadratic equations: formula and roots",
                "type": "video",
                "durationMin": 1,
                "isPreview": true,
                "status": "published",
                "updatedAt": "2026-10-13T12:25:07.891Z",
                "description": "A short silent slide lecture: the standard form, the formula and the discriminant.",
                "objectives": [],
                "content": {
                  "video": {
                    "file": {
                      "id": "edu-media-lecture-quadratic-equations",
                      "name": "quadratic-equations.mp4",
                      "sizeMb": 0.35,
                      "url": "/media/edu-media-lecture-quadratic-equations",
                      "mime": "video/mp4"
                    },
                    "durationSec": 36,
                    "captions": false,
                    "markers": [
                      {
                        "atSec": 12,
                        "title": "The quadratic formula"
                      },
                      {
                        "atSec": 24,
                        "title": "Nature of roots"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                "settings": {}
              },
              {
                "id": "edu-lesson-class-10-maths-factorisation",
                "title": "Solving by factorisation",
                "type": "reading",
                "durationMin": 12,
                "status": "published",
                "updatedAt": "2026-10-13T12:25:07.894Z",
                "objectives": [],
                "content": {
                  "article": {
                    "body": "To factorise x² + bx + c, find two numbers whose product is c and whose sum is b. For x² − 7x + 12 = 0 they are −3 and −4, so (x − 3)(x − 4) = 0 and x = 3 or x = 4.\n\nWhen a ≠ 1, split the middle term using two numbers whose product is ac: 2x² + 7x + 3 = 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = (2x + 1)(x + 3)."
                  }
                },
                "settings": {}
              },
              {
                "id": "edu-lesson-class-10-maths-quadratic-homework",
                "title": "Homework: five quadratic equations",
                "type": "assignment",
                "durationMin": 30,
                "status": "published",
                "updatedAt": "2026-10-13T12:25:07.898Z",
                "objectives": [],
                "content": {
                  "assignment": {
                    "assignmentId": "edu-assignment-quadratic-practice",
                    "brief": "Solve these five equations, showing every step, and state the nature of the roots for each: x² − 5x + 6 = 0; x² + 4x + 4 = 0; 2x² + 7x + 3 = 0; x² − 2x + 5 = 0; 3x² − 12 = 0.",
                    "dueAfterDays": 5,
                    "maxScore": 100,
                    "submission": "text"
                  }
                },
                "settings": {}
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "edu-chapter-class-10-maths-progressions",
            "title": "Arithmetic progressions and trigonometry",
            "summary": "APs, identities and the formula sheet.",
            "objective": "By the end of this chapter, you can find terms and sums of APs and use the trigonometric identities.",
            "status": "published",
            "lessons": [
              {
                "id": "edu-lesson-class-10-maths-ap",
                "title": "Arithmetic progressions",
                "type": "reading",
                "durationMin": 12,
                "status": "published",
                "updatedAt": "2026-10-13T12:25:07.906Z",
                "objectives": [],
                "content": {
                  "article": {
                    "body": "In an AP each term differs from the one before by a fixed common difference d. The nth term is aₙ = a + (n − 1)d, so the 10th term of 3, 7, 11, … is 3 + 9 × 4 = 39.\n\nThe sum of the first n terms is Sₙ = n/2 × [2a + (n − 1)d]. The sum of the first 10 terms of that AP is 5 × (6 + 36) = 210."
                  }
                },
                "settings": {}
              },
